<!-- The .dockerignore file is a configuration file used in Docker to specify files and directories that should be excluded when building a Docker image. It works similarly to a .gitignore file in Git and helps reduce the size of the Docker image by ignoring unnecessary files during the docker build process. -->

<!-- Purpose of .dockerignore

    Reduce Image Size:
    By excluding files that are not required for the application to run (e.g., logs, local development files), the Docker image becomes smaller.

    Improve Build Speed:
    Fewer files mean a faster image build process, as Docker doesn’t have to copy unnecessary files into the image.

    Avoid Sensitive Data in Images:
    Prevents sensitive files (e.g., API keys, credentials, .env files) from being copied into the Docker image.

    Simplify Image Contents:
    Excludes clutter to ensure that the image contains only what’s needed for the application. -->

<!-- Add file or directory patterns to exclude them -->
<!-- Ignore python cache files -->
__pychache__/
*.pyc
*.ipynb
*.ipynb_checkpoints
<!-- Ignore environment files -->
.env

<!-- Ignore log files -->
*.log

<!-- Ignore git repository files -->
.git
.gitignore

<!-- Ignore temporary files -->
tmp/

<!-- Ignore docker related files that are not needed -->
docker-compose.override.yml